earances  there is no body there's no murder  so that is the basis of why  there is such a deep rooted  impunity in thailand and why  torjen for enforcer's appearance  continue to exist  how confident are you that this bill  will pass  previous attempts  to get it through have failed  well  i am  to be honest i will say that i  cautiously welcome the move by the  parliament which  unanimously approved the first reading  of the draft law but the draft itself as  proposed by the government is still very  weak  not in full compliance with  international standard so i hope that  the fitting which just started last week  uh will improve  the language of the of the draft to make  it in line with intellij standard that  is one thing but  equally important  is  why the law hasn't been  in effect yet the thai authorities the  thai government  should demonstrate their commitment and  sincerity in ending in forces appearance  and torture by stop all practices that  lead to  torture and false disappearance so  arbitrary arrest secret detention in  communicate or detention  all these practices need to stop now but  on the contrary no the government  has not listened to calls made by  international and domestic groups that  um police unit  security forces that carry our counter  insurgency in the deep south or  anti-drug units they can no longer hold  anyone that will in secret in  communication okay  so next is  basis of the the public  doubts whether the government is serious  about  ending torture and enforce discipline or  they simply want to have a law that will  be uh shown to the international  community that thailand  has or is going to criminalize culture  and enforce experience but the practice  will continue to be the opposite  okay  thank you for your thoughts sinead pasha  a senior researcher human rights watch  speaking to us there from bangkok  thank you
 

